    {
      "article_id": "4db8847fc63a1b57a8c88f0e",
      "headline": "Intuit Stock's Real Growth Story Isn't The One You're Watching",
      "publisher": "(Trefis)",
      "url": "https://finance.yahoo.com/m/d2a4bdf8-e47f-365c-a531-6152f56bb8a9/intuit-stock%27s-real-growth.html",
      "text": "The market is rightly focused on the company's struggles in do-it-yourself tax, but it might be missing a massive and much more lucrative business taking over from within.\nIf you've held Intuit (INTU) stock over the past year, you're likely feeling the sting. The shares have fallen significantly, trading about 65% below their 52-week high, while the broader market has climbed. The culprit is a familiar story: the company's core do-it-yourself tax business is sputtering at the low end.\nManagement admits it. They're \"constructively dissatisfied\" with performance in the DIY segment and acknowledge they \"lost on price\" among the most price-sensitive filers. It's a real problem, and it's grabbing all the headlines. But what if the most important part of Intuit's story is happening somewhere else entirely?\nA Much Bigger Pond\nWhile the market obsesses over the $5 billion DIY tax market, Intuit has been quietly and aggressively moving into a much larger arena: the $37 billion assisted tax category. This is the world where people pay an expert for the confidence that their taxes are done right, and it accounts for a staggering 88% of the total market.\nThis isn't just a side hustle. It's a strategic takeover. The company's TurboTax Live offering, which connects filers with human experts, is rapidly becoming the main event. And the numbers show a business hitting its stride.\nThe Engine Ignites\nAccording to the company, TurboTax Live revenue is expected to grow 36% this year, with customers growing 38%. That growth is so significant that the assisted offering will represent over half, 53% to be exact, of total TurboTax revenue this year. That's a massive shift, up 11 points from just last year. While one part of the tax franchise is struggling with price, the other, much larger part is booming.\nThis is one of several \"growth engines\" management points to, including its mid-market and money portfolios, that are all growing \"north of 30%.\" The strategy appears to be a deliberate "
    },
